<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cleomenes III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 260–<a href="/years/219-bce">219 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiad dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cleomenes III was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> from about 235 BCE. He cancelled debts, redistributed land and enlarged the citizen body, then fought the Achaean League in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenean-war">Cleomenean War</a>, taking cities such as Tegea. Aratus called in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, who defeated Cleomenes at Sellasia in 222 BCE; he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii.jpg" alt="Cleomenes III" width="320" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Burghers, late 17th century · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kings_Agis_and_Cleomenes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cleomenes was the son of the future Agiad king Leonidas II and his wife Cratesicleia. Around 242 BCE Leonidas was exiled after opposing the reforms of the Eurypontid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agis-iv">Agis IV</a>, but he returned, removed Cleombrotus and had Agis arrested and executed. Cleomenes, then about eighteen, was forced by his father to marry Agis's widow, the wealthy heiress Agiatis. According to legend he was hunting when his father summoned him and learned of the wedding only on reaching the city; he was reportedly doubtful about the marriage because his father had had her husband executed. He came to be inspired by Agis, as told to him by his lover Xenares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Cleomenes took the throne in 235 BCE on his father's death. In 229 BCE Tegea, Mantinea, Caphyae and Orchomenus joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> and William Smith say he took them by treachery, while Richard Talbert says they asked. The Achaean League under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus of Sicyon</a> then declared war. Cleomenes defeated the Achaeans near Mount Lycaeum and again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a>, and, confident of his position, sent followers who killed four of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a>. He then began his reforms, giving his land to the state and dividing all land into equal lots for some 4,000 citizens, half of them old citizens and half new, largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. He restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoge">agoge</a> and the austere communal life and armed his troops with the Macedonian sarissa, a five-metre pike.</p><p>In 226 BCE he removed the Achaean garrison from Mantinea, routed the Achaeans at Dyme and later took Corinth. Oligarchies across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> opposed his reforms. Aratus turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, whose army forced Cleomenes back, and in 222 BCE the Macedonian phalanx overwhelmed the Spartans at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Battle of Sellasia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Sellasia, Cleomenes advised the Spartans to submit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, hoping for help from Ptolemy Euergetes. His successor Ptolemy Philopator neglected him and put him under house arrest. In 219 BCE he and his friends escaped and tried to start a revolt, but found no support, and they killed themselves. Panteus died last, over the king's body. William Smith described Cleomenes as the last truly great man of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and, perhaps with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a>, of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cleomenes III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tensions between rich and poor were acute in the third century BCE, and the programme of <b>Cleomenes III</b> of Sparta, with its promise of debt cancellation and land redistribution, attracted support in several Achaean cities, which helps explain why the league's leaders preferred alliance with Macedon to defeat…</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Antigonus III Doson crushed <b>Cleomenes III</b> of Sparta at the Battle of Sellasia in 222 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The reforming kings Agis IV and <b>Cleomenes III</b> tried to rebuild the citizen body by cancelling debts and redistributing land, but Cleomenes was defeated at Sellasia in 222 BCE by Antigonus III Doson and the Achaean League.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After it joined <b>Cleomenes III of Sparta</b>, the Achaean League and Antigonus Doson destroyed it in 223 BCE and renamed it Antigoneia.</blockquote><a href="/state/mantinea">Mantinea</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cleomenes III of Sparta</b> sacked it in 223 BCE, but it was rebuilt and produced the Achaean leader Philopoemen and the historian Polybius.</blockquote><a href="/state/megalopolis">Megalopolis</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Hellenistic period it passed among the Aetolian League, Sparta under <b>Cleomenes III</b> and the Achaean League, coming under Rome in 146 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/tegea">Tegea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-234" aria-label="Sparta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Cleomenes III"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4485 4380h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4485 4380h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.07,22.43&amp;year=-234">What was here in 234 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
